  "title":"GE-Act 2.0: Pretraining and Scaling a World-Action Model for Robotic Manipulation",
  "title_zh":"GE-Act 2.0：机器人操作世界—动作模型的预训练与规模扩展",
  "abstract":"World-action models predict future states to guide robot actions, enabling learning from both action-free video and action-labeled interaction. Most inherit pretrained video generators, leaving WAM pretraining and scaling underexplored. GE-Act 2.0 initializes all trainable generative and action components from scratch on manipulation data. It combines a control-oriented autoencoder, a single-step visual planner, and an inverse dynamics model, then jointly trains them with knowledge-aligned selective optimization. Checkpoints are evaluated directly without per-task fine-tuning on 100 tasks across 20 skill groups. Scaling co-training data from 300 to 30,000 hours raises success from 17.1% to 44.1% on G1-OP and from 13.4% to 31.1% on G2-90D; despite contributing less than 2% of the data, G2-90D improves by 17.7 points, suggesting cross-embodiment transfer.",
